:"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Halton Council faces \u00a372m five-year budget deficit despite \u00a329.8m savings plan","lead":"Halton Council's five-year financial strategy, presented to the Executive Board on 17 September, forecasts a cumulative budget deficit of \u00a372.567m over the period, even after \u00a329.8m in planned savings. The strategy document states this is \"clearly unsustainable\" and warns that without exceptional financial support (emergency borrowing from government), the chief finance officer must issue a Section 114 notice (a formal warning that freezes new spending), triggering an emergency council meeting within 21 days. The council has used emergency borrowing for three consecutive years: \u00a310m, \u00a314.7m, and \u00a333.873m. By 2031/32, servicing this emergency borrowing alone will cost \u00a327.504m annually\u201412.7% of total council resources. A CIPFA review found the financial position \"extremely fragile\" and concluded the council could not stabilise without continued emergency support.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Halton council faces \u00a373m five-year deficit despite \u00a330m savings plan","lead":"Halton council faces a cumulative budget deficit of \u00a372.6m over five years from 2027/28 onwards, according to its medium-term financial strategy (the council's financial plan for the next three to five years), even after applying \u00a329.8m of planned savings. Every year in the period will run a shortfall. The council plans to cover the gap using Exceptional Financial Support \u2014 emergency government funding for day-to-day running costs \u2014 which it has already drawn \u00a333.9m of in 2026/27. An independent review by CIPFA found the council's position \"extremely fragile\" and said stabilisation was unlikely without continued emergency support. If government does not approve that funding and the council cannot balance its budget, its Section 151 officer (the council's chief finance officer) will have to issue a Section 114 notice, a formal warning that freezes all new spending.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Halton Council faces \u00a372m five-year deficit despite savings plan, raising threat of spending freeze","lead":"Halton Council's Executive Board heard that the authority faces a cumulative budget shortfall of \u00a372.567m over five years, even after delivering a planned savings programme of \u00a329.8m. The council's medium-term financial strategy, covering 2027 to 2031, forecasts deficits in every year. The strategy depends on securing Exceptional Financial Support\u2014government permission to borrow or sell assets to cover day-to-day spending\u2014for a third consecutive year. The council has applied for \u00a333.873m in 2026/27 alone. A CIPFA review commissioned by the council found the financial position \"extremely fragile\" and concluded it is unlikely the council can stabilise its finances without continued support. If the government does not approve the application, the council's chief finance officer must issue a Section 114 notice, which freezes all new spending and requires an emergency meeting within 21 days to agree emergency cuts. The council's cost base is \u00a335m higher annually than comparable councils, forming the basis for the savings target.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Governance Board","paper_title":"Audit and Governance Board - Report September 2026 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83375/83375.pdf","headline":"Toll charges held at \u00a32.40 for Class 2 vehicles at first increase since 2017, well below inflation-adjusted level","detail":"April 2025 marked the first toll charge adjustment since opening in 2017. Despite cumulative inflation over this period, careful financial management resulted in charges held significantly below inflation impact, reflecting conscious policy choice to balance revenue improvement with user affordability.","category":"income","matters":"Demonstrates the council's ability to deliver financial improvements through operational efficiency and traffic growth, reducing reliance on pricing pressure and public friction.","figures":"\u00a32.40 Class 2 toll charge versus \u00a32.90 if full cumulative inflation passed through","quote":"resulting in a \u00a32.40 toll charge for a Class 2 vehicle rather than \u00a32.90 if the full impact of inflation had been passed on to motorists.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Governance Board","paper_title":"Audit and Governance Board - Report September 2026 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83375/83375.pdf","headline":"Toll revenue grew from \u00a344.5m to \u00a359m in four years, a 33% increase driven by traffic recovery and first price rise since 2017","detail":"Project revenue has returned to pre-pandemic levels and continued growing. Traffic crossings increased 3% year-on-year in 2025-26. April 2025 saw the first toll increase since the bridge opened in 2017, carefully managed to protect users.","category":"income","matters":"Strong sustained revenue growth indicates robust demand for the asset and the council's ability to improve financial performance through operational delivery rather than aggressive pricing.","figures":"Actual Revenue \u00a344,453k (2021-22) to \u00a359,025k (2025-26); Traffic crossings up 3% (2025-26 vs 2024-25)","quote":"Project revenue has increased steadily in recent years, returning to pre-pandemic levels in 2023-24 and continuing to grow since then.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Governance Board","paper_title":"Audit and Governance Board - Report September 2026 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83375/83375.pdf","headline":"DfT grant funding requirement declined from \u00a323.7m to \u00a321.8m while unused grant more than doubled from \u00a32.2m to \u00a314.4m","detail":"The Department for Transport grant allocation required each year has fallen as project performance improved. Unused grant returned to DfT has increased substantially, with Halton retaining 15% (\u00a32.2m in 2025-26). This signals declining reliance on government subsidy and generates additional council revenue.","category":"income","matters":"Decreasing grant dependency improves the council's long-term fiscal resilience and demonstrates the project is approaching self-sufficiency.","figures":"DfT Grant Received \u00a323,719k (2021-22) to \u00a321,837k (2025-26); Unused Grant \u00a32,193k to \u00a314,430k; HBC Share @ 15% \u00a3329k (2021-22) to \u00a32,165k (2025-26)","quote":"The grant funding that has been required has been steadily decreasing since 2022-23 for a range of reasons:","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Governance Board","paper_title":"Audit and Governance Board - Report September 2026 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83375/83375.pdf","headline":"The Mersey Gateway Project deficit narrowed from \u00a321.5m in 2021-22 to \u00a37.4m in 2025-26","detail":"Annual expenditure continues to exceed annual income as designed, but the gap has narrowed by 66% over four years. This improvement reflects strong revenue growth, traffic recovery to pre-pandemic levels and beyond, and careful cost management despite inflationary pressures.","category":"capital","matters":"Major capital projects moving toward financial sustainability reduce long-term budget drain on the council and signal improving asset performance to potential lenders.","figures":"Deficit -\u00a321,526k (2021-22) to -\u00a37,407k (2025-26); Actual Revenue \u00a344,453k to \u00a359,025k; Actual Project Costs \u00a355,979k to \u00a366,432k","quote":"as was projected in the project's planning and development phase, annual expenditure exceeds annual income each year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Governance Board","paper_title":"Audit and Governance Board - Report September 2026","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83374/83374.pdf","headline":"Contractual non-compliance in 2025/26 resulted in performance adjustment deductions, despite forecasts assuming 100% compliance.","detail":"During 2025/26, performance adjustment deductions were made for contractual non-compliance. The funding forecasts assume 100% compliance and make no provision for enforcement costs or PCN revenues. The gap between forecast assumptions and actual compliance indicates operational risk.","category":"governance","matters":"Non-compliance with contract performance obligations creates revenue deductions, pressuring project finances at a time when grant is declining and RPI increases are not being implemented.","figures":"Deductions made in 2025/26 (amount not specified)","quote":"During the 2025/26 year deductions were made for contractual non-compliance in relation to the Project Agreement.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Halton UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-27","since_iso":"2026-08-28","distress":true,"headline":"Equal pay claim risk identified from historic task-based payment arrangements, but no provision made; other councils have received claims.","lead":"An unquantified liability is a hidden risk. The council knows other councils have been exposed to this, so the risk is not speculative. If claims are received, they will create unexpected costs that cannot be accommodated in a council this tight on budget.","also":["12 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Governance Board on 2026-09-30","n_findings":13,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","top_paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":87,"news_drafts_total":3}}